A||||12||William||High tech rim painting!||||1||
Z||000000||William||11-01-2000||09:02 PM||||Now that I have refinished my High Tech SE rims, I have decided that the old paint on the inside surface areas need dealing with. Last weekend I stripped the paint off one rim and clear coated the entire thing (leaving the inside parts natural aluminium).<BR>The rim looked really great on the bench, but somehow just seems wrong on the car. The "spoke look" of the rims is now gone and the rim looks rather "busy", if ya know what I mean...I'm now debating if I should repaint the interior areas, but I'm having trouble deciding what I want to do. How about a little vote here guys????? Would your choice be to leave the inside areas natural aluminium, paint them gloss black, or the original color, (I can't really tell what that was?? Whats really got me stumped is how do I paint the inside surfaces without getting it all over the front of the rims? Best I can come up with is to coat the entire front of the rims with a layer of grease to mast it off. Am I on the right track here?<P>Here's the natural look!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/Images/final.JPG"> <p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||11-01-2000||10:10 PM||||Ill be honest, I like that look. If you dont, you could just cut a round peice of cardboard and place behind the spokes and paint the area back there without painting the backside of the spokes. Without the wheel off you'll probally never know the difference. Is that whole wheel natrual aluminum look? I think it looks great.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif"> <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William||11-01-2000||10:26 PM||||Actually, that's not my rim but mine is very simular. My rim is mirror like on the outer flat part and more of of a satin /matte look on the inside...(sort of a two tone aluminium). In the pick I posted,the rim looks all the same. Any more votes?||||reg||1
Z||000003||TigerFiero||11-01-2000||10:32 PM||||I have too just stripped the rims down in the same fashion as you.  I love the look of the all aluminum.  <P>------------------<BR>TigerFiero<BR>86 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||11-01-2000||11:12 PM||||I think they look good like that..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Standard||11-02-2000||01:02 AM||||I'll have to go against the flow here and say that they just don't look quite right.  The "fin" part of the wheel looks out of place and seems to clutter up the wheel when they're not painted.  I think gloss black would make a great contrast, or you could try painting them body color..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||11-02-2000||07:14 AM||||I like the look of the "all-aluminum" rim.  That would be my second choice.  My first choice was to make them look like Formula rims:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Calendar.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||Formula||11-02-2000||08:52 PM||||I like the Formula look best (maybe Im biased LOL), but the all aluminum look is pretty awesome in my book!!  It looks like an aftermarket rim though, it also depends on the color of your car as to what color you do, the all aluminum would look great on a black car but might not be so good on white or grey, red (IMO) looks better with solid black, (biased again)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FormulaSignature2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||2xsess||11-02-2000||09:30 PM||||I think the natural look makes it look kind of fake, like fake gold looks if anyone understands what I mean. <P>I saw Williams wheels last weekend and oh boy do the outer edges look great, like mirrors almost. I think going body color would be too much. I think the original color is like a med gray accent color. You might even be able to go to an auto body supplier and have them look it up for you.<P>That would be my vote. <P>But I'm sure whatever you decide it will look great on that sharp coupe you've got William.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||11-02-2000||10:25 PM||||Personal preference: I like black centers and polished rims, but all-polished looks pretty good, too!  Probably depends on body color.<P>87ECO5 -- What kind of paint did you use?  I'm probably doing the same to mine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||webbee||11-02-2000||10:37 PM||||I vote for the aluminum look because you don't see many Fieros with that style. My secound choice would be to paint the interior with body color.||||reg||1
